/-! ## Records #1416 dropped, restored

#1416 rewrote this slice during a bounded loop run and removed 389 `#print
axioms` records while adding 63 -- a net loss of 380. Nothing left the library
to justify it. `Audit completeness` measured the loss on `main` itself, at
7766d64f:

    AlgebraicGeometry  5982 public,  4899 audited,  1083 missing (ceiling 748)

against 5980 / 5225 / 748 on its parent. `public` rose by TWO while `audited`
fell by 326, so these are declarations that still exist and stopped being
audited -- not code that went away. Every branch cut from `main` inherited the
failure, which is how it was found.

Of the 380, 220 are restored here and 160 are NOT. THE BUILD DECIDED THE SPLIT,
over two iterations, because the obvious offline test was wrong twice.

A first pass restored all 380 on the strength of a grep: 295 names resolved to
a `theorem`/`def`/`structure` declaration directly, and for the other 85 --
projections and auto-generated names like `mk.inj`, `mk.sizeOf_spec` and
structure fields -- the PARENT declaration resolved. That is too weak: a parent
surviving does not mean its field did. `lake env lean` answered with 100
`Unknown constant` errors. Removing those 100 surfaced 60 more, because the
elaborator does not report every error in one pass.

The set is now closed rather than guessed at. Of the 380: 220 appear in the
audit output as `'X' depends on axioms:` or `'X' does not depend on any
axioms`, 160 were reported `Unknown constant`, and ZERO are unaccounted for.
That partition is what this block encodes.

So #1416 legitimately removed 160 declarations and dropping their records was
right; 220 went with them by accident.

`check_audit.py` and the elaborator are the gates that fail on a record
resolving to nothing, and they are what caught both over-restores. That is the
argument for proposing a bulk restore rather than a hand-picked one: being
wrong costs a red build that names every mistake, not a silently widened claim.

They are appended as one block rather than folded back into the sections above,
so the regression and its repair stay legible in the diff. Re-home them by
subject when somebody next edits this slice deliberately.

The same rewrite also left 12 records duplicated verbatim; those second copies
are removed here. A duplicate changes no verdict -- `check_audit_complete.py`
resolves records into a set -- but a file being repaired should not be left
carrying known noise.
-/

